\details{
The chain counter stores one integer \code{cell_count} per region of interest
(ROI). The value \code{-1} marks ROIs of classes that were not configured for chain
counting, \code{0} marks ROIs that were counted but where no cells were detected,
and a positive value is the number of cells in that ROI. Abundance is derived
by translating the values listed in \code{single_cell_values} to a single cell and
using every other value verbatim (see \code{\link[=ifcb_summarize_biovolumes]{ifcb_summarize_biovolumes()}}, which
shares this logic to report \code{cell_counts}).

Chain-length statistics (\code{mean}, \code{median}, \code{max}, \code{sd}) are computed only over
ROIs that were genuinely chain-counted (\code{cell_count >= 1}); ROIs with \code{-1}
(not counted) or \code{0} (no cells detected) are excluded from the length
statistics, although both still contribute to abundance according to
\code{single_cell_values} (by default one cell each).

\code{n_counted} reports how many ROIs those length statistics were computed over,
i.e. the number of ROIs the chain counter measured (\code{cell_count >= 1}). It is
a count of ROIs rather than of chains, and it includes ROIs found to hold a
single cell, which are not chains. It is useful for telling a measured
abundance from an imputed one: a class with \code{cell_counts > 0} but
\code{n_counted == 0} was never chain-counted, so its abundance is one cell per ROI
by imputation rather than by measurement.

Chain counting was introduced by Groves et al. (2026), who trained a
"You Only Look Once" (YOLO) object detection model to enumerate the cells in
diatom chains imaged by the IFCB. The per-ROI \code{cell_count} data summarized
here is produced by the \code{ifcb-pytorch-classify} inference pipeline
(\url{https://github.com/nodc-sweden/ifcb-pytorch-classify}), which writes it
as an optional \code{cell_count} variable in the \code{.mat}, \code{.h5} and \code{.csv}
classification files alongside the class predictions.

This function derives \code{cell_counts} from every classified ROI. This differs
from \code{\link[=ifcb_summarize_biovolumes]{ifcb_summarize_biovolumes()}}, which reports \code{cell_counts} only over ROIs
that also have matching feature (biovolume) data, so the two abundance totals
can differ when some ROIs lack feature data.
